# MARVEL Task 4 – I2C Communication Between Two ESP32 Boards with LCD and Web Servers

## 1. Connections

### ESP32-A (Sender)

| ESP32-A pin | Connection |
|---|---|
| GPIO 21 (SDA) | ESP32-B GPIO 21 (SDA) |
| GPIO 22 (SCL) | ESP32-B GPIO 22 (SCL) |
| GND | ESP32-B GND |
| USB | Connect to computer for programming and Serial Monitor |

### ESP32-B (Receiver + LCD)

| ESP32-B pin | Connection |
|---|---|
| GPIO 21 (SDA) | ESP32-A GPIO 21 (SDA) |
| GPIO 22 (SCL) | ESP32-A GPIO 22 (SCL) |
| GPIO 25 (SDA) | LCD SDA |
| GPIO 26 (SCL) | LCD SCL |
| GND | Common GND with ESP32-A and LCD |
| LCD VCC | Suitable supply for the LCD module, subject to voltage-level safety |

**Important:** Use an appropriate bidirectional I²C level shifter if the LCD backpack's pull-up resistors raise SDA/SCL to 5 V. Do not connect 5 V I²C signals directly to ESP32 GPIOs.

The receiver is configured with I²C address `0x55`, and the LCD address is `0x27`.

## 2. Before Uploading

1. Install the ESP32 board package in Arduino IDE.
2. Use the built-in `WiFi`, `WebServer`, and `Wire` libraries.
3. Set `WIFI_SSID` and `WIFI_PASSWORD` in both sketches to the same Wi-Fi network credentials.
4. Upload Sketch A to ESP32-A.
5. Upload Sketch B to ESP32-B.
6. Open each board's Serial Monitor at **115200 baud** and note the IP addresses printed by the boards.
7. Both boards must be connected to the same Wi-Fi network. If your network uses client isolation, the boards may not be able to communicate over HTTP.

